<article style="position: absolute; left: 820px; top: 64px; width: 628px; height: 854px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; font-family: 'EB Garamond', serif; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.5; color: #3a1c20; text-align: justify; hyphens: auto">
<img src="/coffee/cafe.jpg" alt="the coffee counter at a cafe, chalkboard menus and warm pendant lights" style="display: block; flex: none; width: 628px; height: 240px; object-fit: cover; object-position: center 40%">
<h2 style="margin: 22px 0 16px; font-family: 'EB Garamond', serif; font-style: italic; font-weight: 400; font-size: 32px; line-height: 1.1; text-align: left; color: #3a1c20">i like to hang out alone at a crowded café. <span style="color: #6E1E2B">but why?</span></h2>
<div style="flex: none; column-count: 2; column-gap: 48px">
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">there’s a table in every café i love. it’s never the best one. it’s the one by the wall, where i can see the door, the counter and the barista’s mood all at once. i order something i don’t need, open a book i won’t finish, and stay for three hours.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">if you live in bangalore, you already know this tribe. laptops at 10am, a cold brew sweating next to a pitch deck. someone once told me the whole café-as-office thing started here. i believed it instantly. even founders must get bored of their own company, <em>i thought to myself.</em></p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">so i joined them. it was hard at first. <em>i like to read a room. any room.</em> and a café is a room that never stops talking. the couple pretending they aren’t fighting. the man rehearsing an interview under his breath. the girl laughing so hard she has to put her coffee down.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-style: italic; color: #6E1E2B; text-align: left; break-inside: avoid">somewhere between the second coffee and the third chapter, i stopped being distracted and started feeling held.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">at kohe in hyderabad, it’s never quiet. friends arguing over the bill, young parents stealing an hour back, families home after years abroad, ordering everything because they can. i spot more stories there in one afternoon than i could write in a year.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">that’s the thing about being alone in a crowded place. no one needs anything from you. you’re not lonely, you’re just un-asked. the noise keeps you company without asking you to perform.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">and bangalore, i’ve come to realise, is a city of choices. people here live like adults. maybe that’s why this caught on: sitting alone in a full café is a quiet way of saying <em>i chose this.</em> no plans, no explanations, no one to answer to but the menu.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px; color: #6E1E2B">so tell me, where do you go to be alone, and who do you watch while you’re there?</p>
